From: GoProSlowYo <68455785+goproslowyo@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 21:25:11 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 19/21] dndcp: Make host to guest drags reach Wayland
 applications on GNOME

GNOME has no layer shell, so the native drag source is not available
there, and a host to guest drag stays on XDND from the Xwayland
detection window. Mutter bridges such a drag to Wayland clients, but
only under conditions the plugin did not meet. Every drop onto a Wayland
application such as Nautilus was lost, with stock open-vm-tools as well.

Mutter starts the bridge when an X client takes XdndSelection, and only
if a pointer then has a button held over an Xwayland window. It then
follows its own pointer and maps an XDND proxy window over whichever
Wayland window is under it. At the start of each drag the plugin now
checks for a Wayland session without the native source whose X window
manager is Mutter. It finds the window manager the EWMH way, through
_NET_SUPPORTING_WM_CHECK and the _NET_WM_NAME that Mutter sets to "GNOME
Shell" or "Mutter", and reads it again for every drag, since the window
manager can be replaced. Every other session keeps the upstream XDND
behaviour. Under Mutter the plugin does the following.

It asks for _NET_WM_STATE_ABOVE on the detection window, which stays
managed. GNOME keeps a window that was mapped without focus below the
focused one, so the press landed on whatever the user had last clicked.

It presses in the middle of the window where the compositor put it, and
does not move the window to the press first. It presses only once the X
server reports the pointer at that spot and over the window. A press
anywhere else lands on the window underneath and starts moving it. If
the pointer does not get there, the plugin does not press, and tries
again on the host's next motion, up to three times.

It moves across the window in small steps from the main loop, because
GTK does not start a drag from motion that arrives all at once. The
pointer stays on the window until 300 ms after GTK has started the drag,
so that Mutter finds it there when it looks. Leaving at once works only
while the pointer's implicit grab holds it on the window, which it no
longer does after any click on a Wayland window.

It does not warp the X pointer. Xwayland carries out a warp from the
client under the pointer with a pointer lock, and Mutter's pointer then
stays on the detection window while only Xwayland's moves.

It releases the button once the target has accepted the drag, or after
1.5 s, instead of as soon as the host drops. An accept counts only from
100 ms after the pointer has left the detection window. The window is a
drop target too, for guest to host drags, so it answers the drag while
it starts over it, and GTK keeps that answer until the next target
replies. A drop with no GTK drag to wait for, because GTK never started
it or it has already ended, releases at once.

It ignores a GTK drag from an earlier host drag, at its begin and at its
end. When the host cancels before GTK has run the main loop, the old
press can start a GTK drag after the next host drag has begun. A drop
that the target never accepted can stay alive in GTK until the next drag
begins, and resetting on its end released the new drag's press.

It keeps the detection window mapped while a drag is still starting from
it or waiting to release. When the host's files arrive the common layer
returns to READY and hides the window, and on a quick drop that happened
before the release, which took the drag's origin away.

For host to guest drags, a test program drove the plugin's own detection
window and uinput code through this sequence on GNOME 50.3 (Bluefin 44),
and 199 of 200 runs landed the drop. Host drags into Nautilus were then
tested by hand with the final code.
